WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.190
Now, if I may, you're Sunshine Girl,

2
00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.430
I love the cheerfulness of that.

3
00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:10.130
I march in the army of optimism

4
00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.650
and I am looking for recruits.

5
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.870
That being said, I have tried my whole life

6
00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.330
to figure out what true strength is.

7
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.170
And when I look at your life,

8
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.600
I have some questions for you

9
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.110
because I have a deep admiration

10
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.900
from bold decisions that you've made in your life.

11
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.940
So all practical jokes and sunshine and optimism aside.

12
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.680
How in the heck did you find your strength

13
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.360
to make decisions like,

14
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.510
I have this incredible paycheck that's coming to me,

15
00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.640
it's for two years of work,

16
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.840
but I'm not happy inside, so I'm gonna walk away.

17
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.070
What advice do you have for people

18
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.150
to not live their authentic life

19
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.690
and fight for their truth at whatever cost, pun intended.

20
00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.440
Yeah, so I mean, it wasn't an easy decision for me,

21
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.060
obviously, I was set to do my six year contract on "ER."

22
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.700
It had all been planned out

23
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.510
even with George's character coming back

24
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.060
to get me on my last episode,

25
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.032
we had planned all that out with the producers in secret

26
00:01:11.032 --> 00:01:15.260
and then all of a sudden, money came into it.

27
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.332 line:15%
Suddenly they said, well, if you stay for two years,

28
00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:19.780 line:15%
we'll give you $27 million.

29
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.750 line:15%
And I am very aware that most people think I'm crazy

30
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.040
and that's okay, I understand why, I get it.

31
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.300
But for me,

32
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.193
I felt I was 32,

33
00:01:33.442 --> 00:01:37.540
I owned my house outright, no mortgage.

34
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.860
I was able to pay both my mother and my father's mortgages.

35
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.190
I had not a lot,

36
00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.300
but a substantial amount of money in the bank, enough.

37
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.130
I didn't have children, I wasn't married.

38
00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:52.180
Wasn't this what I worked six years on a phenomenal show,

39
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.810
had an incredible experience.

40
00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.740
And everyone's argument to staying was,

41
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.010
you'll never have to work again,

42
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.520
which I found to be a very strange argument

43
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.383
because I love working, it's who I am.

44
00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.810
I always say, go with your heart, never your bank account,

45
00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.580
because your bank account can't make you happy,

46
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.180
only in here can make you happy.

47
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.120
There is a story, if I may,

48
00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.640
that happened with Steven Seagal,

49
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.890
and you had an incident where he had asked you

50
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.053
to come to his hotel room for casting.

51
00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:32.500
And you thought the casting director would be there.

52
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.700
The reason I wanted to ask you this story

53
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.160
was because this is a part of the strength

54
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.180
I admire in you so much.

55
00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.570
I think you have these incredible instincts for life.

56
00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.230
Yeah, so it was actually the casting director

57
00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.837
that asked me to go to his hotel room.

58
00:02:46.837 --> 00:02:49.290
I had a fourth and final call back the next day,

59
00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.707
and she called me to say,

60
00:02:50.707 --> 00:02:52.270
"He'd like to run the scene with you,

61
00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.360
he saw your tape, he thinks you're great.

62
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.220
Come to the hotel room."

63
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.100
And I didn't wanna go because I lived in Brooklyn,

64
00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.960
I couldn't afford a cab, I was waitressing, I was broke.

65
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.660
And she said, "Don't worry, we'll pay for the car,

66
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.500
get a car service, and I'll be here,

67
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:06.980
we're gonna run the scene."

68
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.720
So I did that.

69
00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.923
And when I got to his hotel room,

70
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:13.863
the casting director was not there.

71
00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:19.770
And it was a frightening moment because he asked me

72
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.360
to sit on a couch and underneath this cushion,

73
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.720
I jumped up, there was something hard

74
00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.647
and he pulled out a gun and said,

75
00:03:26.647 --> 00:03:29.260
"Oh, I must've left my gun there."

76
00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:34.260
I'm 23, and I realized I'm in an action star's hotel room

77
00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:37.943
with a gun, I'd never seen a gun before.

78
00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:40.523
And he's six foot four.

79
00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.080
And no one knows where I am.

80
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.791
There's no cell phones.

81
00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.420
The boyfriend I was living with at the time,

82
00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.210
he was at work, I hadn't called him

83
00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.130
to let him know where I was going.

84
00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:55.530
And so, anyway, the way I diffused the situation

85
00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.460
was I started thinking fast on my feet

86
00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.510
and I was a tough New Yorker, but what did it for me,

87
00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.270
he asked me to come into the bedroom 'cause he was tired

88
00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.570
and he asked to read my palm.

89
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.210
And I was like, okay.

90
00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:10.130
And he put the gun down,

91
00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.530
so I was calming down a little bit.

92
00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.263
And he read my palm and told me that he was a healer.

93
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:19.560
And that I had weak kidneys.

94
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.560
And I thought, is this the shtick that works on women?

95
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:26.080
This is ridiculous.

96
00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.920
And I thought, oh my God, he thinks this is going to

97
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.520
make me think he's some sort of almighty healer and stay.

98
00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.439
Anyway, he read the next palm and got in there, whatever,

99
00:04:37.439 --> 00:04:39.770
got in there and started giving me more advice

100
00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:43.030
about my body and how I could fix it through his healing.

101
00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:47.300
The tough New Yorker in me just inwardly was laughing

102
00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.300
because it was so awful and hilarious

103
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.810
that I just sort of stood up and I went well, that was fun,

104
00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.270
thanks about the kidneys, got to go.

105
00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:59.560
And I just ran for the door and, you know, waving,

106
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.030
see you tomorrow, can't wait.

107
00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:02.493
And I got out of there.

108
00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.724
So I got to the elevator and realized

109
00:05:05.724 --> 00:05:07.410
he hadn't reimbursed me for the money

110
00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.390
and I was really broke and it was midnight now.

111
00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:12.110
And there was no way I could take a subway home

112
00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:12.983
from Midtown.

113
00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:17.320
And I went back and knocked on the door,

114
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.030
but I made sure to stay on the other side of the door.

115
00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.640
And I spoke really loudly,

116
00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.870 line:15%
and he sort of had his Cheshire Cat grin.

117
00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:28.178 line:15%
And I said, "I was told I'd be reimbursed for the car fare,

118
00:05:28.178 --> 00:05:30.680 line:15%
so you need to reimburse me for the car fare."

119
00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.720
And he reached into his pocket and handed me money,

120
00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.980
I didn't look, I ran to the elevator, got in the elevator,

121
00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.270
looked at my palm.

122
00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.150
He had given me a $100 bill.

123
00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.600
I had never seen a $100 bill.

124
00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.080
And I figured if I just survived in a hotel room

125
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.610
with an action star and a gun without getting hurt,

126
00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.480
I could take a subway ride home and pay off my credit card.

127
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:49.570
So that's what I did.

128
00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:52.200
I took the subway home and paid off my credit card.

129
00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.443
And that is storytelling.

130
00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:58.490
And we have reached out to Steven Seagal

131
00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.910
and his reps came back saying

132
00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.800
that he denies this ever happened, so.

133
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.550
I appreciated that story so much

134
00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:09.180
as someone who has been put in those situations too

135
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.720
and just been like, you're absolutely kidding me,

136
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.550
I'm walking out of here.

137
00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.362
And I just appreciated that story.

138
00:06:17.362 --> 00:06:18.400
Thank you.

139
00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.070 line:15%
I appreciate so many things about you.

140
00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:23.860 line:15%
The choices you have made,

141
00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.870 line:15%
the way you've lived your life on your own terms,

142
00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:30.890
the life that you've had, the person you've become,

143
00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.860
the way you've navigated your family, your career.

144
00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.210
And I can't thank you enough

145
00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.130
for this wonderful conversation,

146
00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:39.670
all the time you've given.

147
00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:41.860
Thank you, Julianna Margulies.

148
00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.190
Thank you for having me, Drew, it was so nice,

149
00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:44.850
and congratulations on your show.

150
00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.057
I'm so happy for you.

151
00:06:46.057 --> 00:06:49.373
"Sunshine Girl: An Unexpected Life" is out now.

152
00:06:49.373 --> 00:06:51.956 line:15%
